{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-1 (27cb5f60): before the code, answers to ruling 1064d0de, read at main 324a0679.\n\nToday. Only the opt-in counts bytes. `#full` (scope/src/turn.ts:466-474) sends a scope without `whole-scope` to `fits` (derive/src/reserve.ts:235-250), which counts entries only. The opt-in calls `logicalBytes()` (scope/src/sqlite.ts:369-388), a full scan that groups every `entry`, `retained_input`, `retained_value` and `accepted_key` row on each judged write. `capacityFull` (reserve.ts:256-273) then answers `scopeBytes`. Births and copies are `retained_input` rows of kind `entry`. `retain` (sqlite.ts:672-680) is the only code that inserts them, and `retainBirth` calls it.\n\n1. A stored total. A scan of `retained_input WHERE kind = 'entry'` on each write reads one row per retained copy and birth, so the cost per input grows with the very quantity that the cap bounds. Proposal: one `meta` row, `retained-entry-bytes`. `retain` adds the size of an `entry` row only when its INSERT inserts a row (ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING adds nothing). `forget` subtracts for kind `entry`; today `forget` runs only for `text` (turn.ts:521). Each input reads one row, and an input that retains a new row writes one more. The total updates inside the seal transaction, so `Full` rolls it back with the entry. The row stays outside the folded state and the state digest, and replay never reads it. This adds stored state, so the head lands before the cutover deployment. The opt-in keeps `logicalBytes()`, which already counts these rows.\n\n2. A scope made before the head. The `SqliteStore` constructor fills the row with one scan when the row is absent, beside the `pass` column migration at sqlite.ts:161-164. A scope with no rows gets 0. The first turn sees the true total. After that, only `retain` and `forget` change the row.\n\n3. A scope past the cap. Only a scope made before the head can be past it. The stop applies to an input that adds new `entry` bytes and leaves the total above `scopeBytes`. `#seal` returns the bytes it added, and `#full` checks them after `fits` passes. These get the stop:\n- create and genesis: refused `scope-full` (core.ts:616);\n- an act: refused `scope-full` (core.ts:775);\n- a prepare: refused `scope-full` (core.ts:871);\n- a delivery: `retry` `scope-full`, and the sender keeps the task (delivery.ts:313);\n- an outcome with new births: `unavailable` `scope-full` (operations.ts:810).\nEach site adds `name: bound` only under `capacity`, so each gains `|| bound === \"scopeBytes\"`. Reads, a floor, a checkpoint, a timed turn and any input that only reuses retained sequences still pass. No input frees this space: a floor deletes no birth or copy (sqlite.ts:325-349), so a scope past the cap stays past it.\n\nThe head also changes the comment at contract/src/bounds.ts:66 (\"whole-scope opt-in only\"). Witness: a scope without the opt-in, with a small `scopeBytes`, retains births across a floor, and its next new birth gets `scope-full` with `scopeBytes`. The witness fails at 57e893c0.\n\nThree questions for a ruling:\na. A delivery that settles a request of this scope (a result, or control) gets `retry` in a scope past the cap, so the request never settles. Does such a delivery pass the stop, or does the stop hold for every delivery?\nb. The count covers only `entry` rows, the births and copies. Without the opt-in, `rule`, `snapshot`, `definition` and `value` rows also have no byte cap. Proposal: leave them out, as the ruling names births and copies only.\nc. The count leaves journal bytes out in scopes without the opt-in. Those scopes allow up to `scopeEntries` × `entryBytes` of journal. Counting the journal would make scopes that work today refuse. Proposal: leave the journal out."}